<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.17.1: Quadratic Expressions in Different Forms</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02045</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.17.1: Quadratic Expressions in Different Forms</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c2a605eb-80ad-49fe-a8ba-2f0b47ee672f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up</title>
      <para id="para-00001">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_19_02_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00002">Here are graphs representing the functions \(f\) and \(g\) given by \(f(x)=x(x+6)\) and \(g(x)=x(x+6)+4\).</para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Which graph represents \(f(x)=x(x+6)\)? How do you know?</para>
            <para id="para-00004">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00006">The lower graph (which goes through the origin) is the graph of \(f\).</para>
            <list id="list-00001">
              <item>The value of \(g\) is always 4 greater than the value of \(f\), no matter which value of \(x\) we choose.</item>
              <item>Rewriting the equation in standard form gives \(f(x)=x^2+6x\). This form shows that the \(y\)-intercept of the graph representing \(f\) is \((0,0)\).</item>
              <item>The expression that defines function \(f\) is in factored form. It shows that the two \(x\)-intercepts are \((0,0)\) and \((-6,0)\).</item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">Which graph represents \(g(x)=x(x+6)+4\)? How do you know?</para>
            <para id="para-00008">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00009">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00010"><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The lower graph (which goes through the origin) is the graph of \(f\) and the upper one is the graph of \(g\).</para>
            <list id="list-00002">
              <item>The value of \(g\) is always 4 greater than the value of \(f\), no matter which value of \(x\) we choose.</item>
              <item>Rewriting the equation in standard form gives \(g(x)=x^2+6x+4\).This form shows that the \(y\)-intercept of the graph representing \(g\) is \((0,4)\).</item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Where does the graph of \(f\) meet the \(x\)-axis? Explain how you know.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00013">\(x=-6\) and \(x=0\), because these are the two values of \(x\) that make \(x(x+6)\)  equal to 0.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content></document>